## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to design per-tenant traffic control across data centers, implement dynamic configuration updates, and establish observability—all without service downtime.

## Key Concepts
- **Deterministic hashing**: Ensures consistent per-tenant canary decisions across all service instances
- **Docker Config**: Enables dynamic configuration reloads and zero-downtime rollouts
- **Multi-DC routing**: Traefik handles TLS termination with per-tenant observability across both data centers

## Code Example
```javascript
function isCanary(tenant, percentage) {
  const hash = [...tenant].reduce((acc, char) => (acc * 31 + char.charCodeAt(0)) >>> 0, 0);
  return (hash % 100) < percentage;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a policy-driven, per-tenant response shaping mechanism in a docker-dca setup, using a Config object propagated to the service. It demonstrates practical data-safety concerns and integration with Traefik TLS termination.

## Key Concepts

- Docker Config for policy data
- Per-tenant response shaping and JSON filtering
- Payload transformation without altering business logic
- Traefik TLS edge in multi-DC deployments
- Basic end-to-end testing in a docker-dca context

## Code Example

```go
// Go example: filterFields filters payload by allowed keys
func filterFields(in map[string]interface{}, allowed []string) map[string]interface{} {
    out := make(map[string]interface{})
    set := make(map[string]struct{}, len(allowed))
    for _, k := range allowed { set[k] = struct{}{} }
    for k, v := range in {
        if _, ok := set[k]; ok {
            out[k] = v
        }
    }
    return out
}
```

